---
/**
 * The company name, set as type — never an image, never a logo mark, never a heading
 * (AAP 0.2.2, 0.8.1).
 *
 * The brand is authored as type because rule W-1 keeps the design file's own logo mark and
 * wordmark glyphs out of the output, so there is no mark to place: these words are the whole
 * identity the component renders. Nothing is imported, no image element exists, and the family
 * is Urbanist, declared once in the theme and served from this origin (rule B-1).
 *
 * It renders a <p>: a block box, so the name can take the hero's two lines and the footer's one
 * without any element needing to be a heading. Each page's single <h1> is the hero headline
 * `Hero.astro` renders after this element, so no heading level belongs here.
 *
 * The ink is inherited rather than set. The theme paints the page Chocolate on Cream (17.08:1),
 * and `currentColor` carries whatever ink the block this is composed into sets — Cream inside the
 * two dark bands, whose `.tone-inverse` rule does exactly that. Alignment is the consumer's too:
 * the About page's top block right-aligns the wordmark and the footer left-aligns it, so no
 * `text-align` is written here.
 *
 * It is not interactive: no link, no tab stop, no hover, focus or active state, and no script —
 * server-rendered markup only (rule W-2).
 */
type Place = 'hero' | 'footer';

interface Props {
  /**
   * Where the wordmark sits, which fixes the line limit it must fit: the hero is measured
   * against two lines and the footer against one (AAP 0.2.2). It changes nothing else — not the
   * family, the weight, the rung or the ink.
   */
  place: Place;
  /**
   * The company name exactly as A0 writes it, passed from `global.wordmark` (AAP 0.7.2).
   *
   * An extension to the interface AAP 0.8.1 declares for this component: a component renders a
   * string only from a prop or an expression over props (AAP 0.4.3), and the name is copy the
   * content layer owns, so it arrives here instead of being typed in this file. That keeps
   * `src/content/global.ts` the one definition the header and the footer both read.
   */
  text: string;
}

const { place, text } = Astro.props;

/**
 * The display rung each place takes, from the fit measurement in AAP 0.2.2.
 *
 * Both places take `text-display-2` — Urbanist 500, line-height 1, at the token's mobile step
 * below the theme's 900-pixel breakpoint and its desktop step above it — because the
 * 21-character name sets on a single line inside both content columns: it fills 83 per cent of
 * the mobile column and 65 per cent of the desktop one. That satisfies the hero's two-line limit
 * and the footer's one-line limit in both places, so neither steps down and the two places share
 * one rung. At the 320-pixel reflow width the same setting wraps to two lines instead of
 * overflowing the column, which is what keeps the page free of horizontal scroll below the
 * design widths.
 *
 * The step exists for the case the measurement rules out: were the setting to wrap inside the
 * footer's one-line limit, the footer alone would take `type-heading-2`, the next rung down the
 * 390 ladder, while the hero kept `text-display-2` over its two lines. A rung is only ever
 * chosen from the scale — no size invented between rungs, and none below the smallest one.
 */
const RUNG: Record<Place, string> = {
  hero: 'type-display-2',
  footer: 'type-display-2',
};
---

<p class={RUNG[place]}>{text}</p>
